A signed graph is a pair Σ = (G, σ) where G = (V, E) is a finite simple graph and σ : E → {+1, −1} is a sign function on its edges. The sign of a cycle (circle) in Σ is the product of the signs of its edges; a cycle is positive if its sign is +1 and negative if its sign is −1. The signed graph Σ is called balanced if every cycle in Σ is positive, and unbalanced otherwise. A Hamiltonian circle in Σ is a cycle in the underlying graph G that visits every vertex of V exactly once.

Suppose Σ is an unbalanced signed graph that contains at least one Hamiltonian circle. Determine, in general:

(a) whether Σ must contain a negative Hamiltonian circle;
(b) whether Σ must contain a positive Hamiltonian circle;
(c) whether Σ must contain both a positive and a negative Hamiltonian circle.

Provide either a proof of existence in each case or a counterexample (an unbalanced signed graph having a Hamiltonian circle but lacking the corresponding signed Hamiltonian circle).

